n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b4edd8ed1597cb536cb9241b96b7c0a341fd4c9f62309143d81a8332951561b"} {"question" "q-95386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95386" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}^{200}$\n- $R[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 200$ matrices over $R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $R[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{a}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${a}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $R[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $R[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200}(R)$ and ${R}^{200}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200}(R))={200}^{2}=40\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{200})=200\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b4edd8ed1597cb536cb9241b96b7c0a341fd4c9f62309143d81a8332951561b"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95239" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $1\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 500$ matrices over $R$\n- $C,$ as a vector space over $R$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}_{1000}[t]$ and ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{1000}[t])=1\\,000+1=1\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 500}(R))=200\\cdot 500=100\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C$ is also finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of complex numbers is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,i}\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1+b\\cdot i=0\n$$\n\nif and only if $a=0$ and $b=0.$ Since our vector space is also spanned by this linearly independent subset, we have that $\\text{dim}(C)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b4edd8ed1597cb536cb9241b96b7c0a341fd4c9f62309143d81a8332951561b"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "q-95240" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/state.json" "file_sha256" "413fcdf6e3e7078d81e39d698dd046ae900e748b0d4d52c23c2b7257bd22941f"} {"question" "e-8288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8288.json" "file_sha256" "b8aa19f68aecbb42d86b2ead9aa70b10868315ba703a0a8ee440f022c966e650"} {"question" "e-8288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8288.json" "file_sha256" "b8aa19f68aecbb42d86b2ead9aa70b10868315ba703a0a8ee440f022c966e650"} {"question" "e-8315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8315.json" "file_sha256" "229664741c3aee972bd464c9aa90960775bcab87443c16eb015aa5d284d6f298"} {"question" "e-8315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/assets/f2ab6a78388c9be5a4b46d63916c8e299cb95746547189ed9fe2783938c007dc.png)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8315.json" "file_sha256" "229664741c3aee972bd464c9aa90960775bcab87443c16eb015aa5d284d6f298"} {"question" "e-8289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8289.json" "file_sha256" "ea1af3883eda69ae4726bc8d4e49c22e6e46d3dd93b6cb8b401e8e6373072842"} {"question" "e-8289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8289.json" "file_sha256" "ea1af3883eda69ae4726bc8d4e49c22e6e46d3dd93b6cb8b401e8e6373072842"} {"question" "e-8290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\n- $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8290.json" "file_sha256" "94fda5350182de5037adbac5f4275036a2083a98a9675148d68e9fc199e1a736"} {"question" "e-8290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072692/example-8290.json" "file_sha256" "94fda5350182de5037adbac5f4275036a2083a98a9675148d68e9fc199e1a736"}] "usage" {"q-36471" [] "e-8288" [] "e-8289" [] "e-8290" [] "e-8315" []} "basis" 2649 "retractions" [[17592186152501 :question/problem "What is the dimension of $R_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7$?"] [17592186151956 :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6$?"] [17592186151956 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1\n$$"] [17592186151959 :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$?"] [17592186151959 :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1$.\n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 2= 6\n$$\n\nelements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$.\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m\n$$"] [17592186151957 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9$?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n2. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n3. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186151957 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n1. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n2. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n3. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2$. Therefore, $\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186151958 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n2. $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n3. $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$"] [17592186151958 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n$\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100$\nand\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000$\nare both finite numbers.\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n$\\big\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N$.\nIndeed, we have that\n$\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0$\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n$. Therefore, for any $n\\in \\mathbb N$, there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n$, which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186149917 17592186149918 17592186149919 17592186149920 17592186149921] "immutable_fields" [[17592186149916 :answer-field/correct] [17592186149916 :answer-field/id] [17592186149916 :answer-field/key] [17592186149916 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
